    "body": "I think this should even apply for `<link>` elements, although it's pretty theoretical. The idea would be if a browser used e.g. `<link rel=\"next\" href=\"...\" referrerpolicy=\"..\">` to display a \"Go to next page\" navigation UI, this \"follows a hyperlink\" algorithm would apply. In that case I think referrerpolicy should still work.\n\nThis would mean you'd need to change `<code data-x=\"attr-hyperlink-referrerpolicy\">referrerpolicy</code>` to `<code data-x=\"\">referrerpolicy</code>`, which is a bit sad, but it's something we do elsewhere for attributes shared between multiple elements.\n",
    "created_at": "2016-08-11T15:43:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-11T21:33:34Z",
